WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.222
Defensive Rust starts with representing
the problem clearly.

2
00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:04.997
Types are not just containers for data;

3
00:00:05.077 --> 00:00:07.646
they are a way to express what the program
believes is valid.

4
00:00:07.726 --> 00:00:09.986
A raw string or integer can carry almost
any meaning.

5
00:00:10.066 --> 00:00:12.120
A domain type can carry a much narrower
meaning,

6
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.282
and that narrower meaning makes misuse
easier to notice during review.

7
00:00:15.362 --> 00:00:18.957
Newtypes are useful when values share a
primitive representation but should not be

8
00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:19.859
interchangeable.

9
00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:22.045
User IDs, tenant IDs, file names, tokens,
paths,

10
00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:25.104
and request identifiers may all appear as
strings in external input,

11
00:00:25.184 --> 00:00:28.780
but they usually have different validation
rules and different security meaning.

12
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.507
Giving them separate types helps the
compiler and the reviewer see mistakes sooner.

13
00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:33.922
Enums can model states, modes,

14
00:00:34.002 --> 00:00:36.519
and intent without scattering flags across
the codebase.

15
00:00:36.599 --> 00:00:38.859
A job can be pending, running, completed,
or failed.

16
00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:41.148
A request can be unauthenticated,
authenticated,

17
00:00:41.228 --> 00:00:42.820
or authorized for a specific action.

18
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.547
Encoding meaningful states directly
prevents impossible combinations from being

19
00:00:46.627 --> 00:00:47.654
represented casually.

20
00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:49.943
Option and Result are also defensive
design tools.

21
00:00:50.023 --> 00:00:53.516
Option makes absence explicit, so callers
cannot pretend a value always exists.

22
00:00:53.596 --> 00:00:56.575
Result makes failure explicit, so code has
to decide how to handle it.

23
00:00:56.655 --> 00:01:00.250
Constructors and parsers can validate data
at the boundary and return domain types

24
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.717
that downstream code can trust.

25
00:01:01.797 --> 00:01:04.571
This approach reduces repeated checks and
makes review easier.

26
00:01:04.651 --> 00:01:08.092
Instead of asking whether every call site
remembered the same validation rule,

27
00:01:08.172 --> 00:01:11.305
reviewers can look at where a type is
constructed and what it guarantees.

28
00:01:11.385 --> 00:01:15.186
Clear data models turn defensive
programming from scattered caution into enforceable

29
00:01:15.266 --> 00:01:15.780
structure.
